{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789173672,"nonce":"ZBflSajX+7CLEFC4yqimv0aCrB4=","title":"`==` on a String field read off an erased generic's anon element compares unequal","message":"The value is correct -- it concatenates and prints as \"rr\" -- but every\nequality on it is false. /tmp/probe/LCMP2.hx:\n\n    private typedef Tok = { p:String, s:Bool };\n    var lt = new List\u003cTok\u003e(); lt.add({p:\"rr\", s:true});\n    lt.first().p == \"rr\"                      // FALSE   (want true)\n    var s = lt.first().p; s == \"rr\"           // FALSE   (want true)\n\n    var ls = new List\u003cString\u003e(); ls.add(\"hello\");\n    ls.first() == \"hello\"                     // true    -- String itself is fine\n    var t:Tok = {p:\"rr\", s:true}; t.p == \"rr\" // true    -- plain anon is fine\n    [{p:\"rr\", s:true}][0].p == \"rr\"           // true    -- through Array is fine\n\nSo it is specifically an anon object that came back through the erased\ngeneric return unboxing (8cd8b649 / 091476f0). That unboxing bitcasts the\nrecovered pointer to `convert_type(Tok)` = Ptr(Void). A field read on a\nPtr(Void)-typed register evidently takes a different path from one on a\nproperly anon-typed register: the String it yields prints (the Dynamic-aware\nstringify reads it fine) but the `==` lowering compares it as something other\nthan a String -- by pointer, or as an erased i64.\n\nBefore those commits `.p` on this value read the box's type tag (3), so this is\nstrictly less wrong, but it is a silent wrong answer and a `!=` guard on it\ntakes the wrong branch.\n\nSuggested direction: after the unbox, type the register as the anon's own\nIrType rather than Ptr(Void), or make the anon field read on a Ptr(Void)\nreceiver produce a String-typed register when the declared field type is\nString.","files":null}]}